  [
    {
      formula      => "C_4H_10",
      formulaHTML  => "C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>",
      rank         => "highest",
      rankNumber   => "1 - highest boiling point",
      reason       => "C4H10 is the largest hydrocarbon in this set, so it has the strongest London dispersion forces and the highest boiling point.",
    },
    {
      formula      => "C_3H_8",
      formulaHTML  => "C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>",
      rank         => "middle",
      rankNumber   => "2 - middle boiling point",
      reason       => "C3H8 is larger than C2H6 but smaller than C4H10, so its London dispersion forces are in the middle for this set.",
    },
    {
      formula      => "C_2H_6",
      formulaHTML  => "C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>",
      rank         => "lowest",
      rankNumber   => "3 - lowest boiling point",
      reason       => "C2H6 is the smallest hydrocarbon in this set, so it has the weakest London dispersion forces and the lowest boiling point.",
    },
  ],

  [
    {
      formula      => "C_4H_10",
      formulaHTML  => "C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>",
      rank         => "highest",
      rankNumber   => "1 - highest boiling point",
      reason       => "C4H10 is the largest hydrocarbon in this set, so it has the strongest London dispersion forces and the highest boiling point.",
    },
    {
      formula      => "C_2H_6",
      formulaHTML  => "C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>",
      rank         => "middle",
      rankNumber   => "2 - middle boiling point",
      reason       => "C2H6 is larger than CH4 but smaller than C4H10, so its London dispersion forces are in the middle for this set.",
    },
    {
      formula      => "CH_4",
      formulaHTML  => "CH<sub>4</sub>",
      rank         => "lowest",
      rankNumber   => "3 - lowest boiling point",
      reason       => "CH4 is the smallest hydrocarbon in this set, so it has the weakest London dispersion forces and the lowest boiling point.",
    },
  ],
);

############################################################
# Choose one set and randomize display order
############################################################

$setIndex = random(0, scalar(@sets) - 1, 1);

@selected = @{ $sets[$setIndex] };

# Randomize display order
@selected = random_subset(3, @selected);

############################################################
# Dropdown menus
############################################################

@rankOptions = (
  "1 - highest boiling point",
  "2 - middle boiling point",
  "3 - lowest boiling point",
);

@rankMenus = map {
  PopUp(
    ["", @rankOptions],
    $_->{rankNumber}
  )
} @selected;

############################################################
# Find indices for solution table
############################################################

@highest_indices = grep { $selected[$_]->{rank} eq "highest" } 0..$#selected;
@middle_indices  = grep { $selected[$_]->{rank} eq "middle"  } 0..$#selected;
@lowest_indices  = grep { $selected[$_]->{rank} eq "lowest"  } 0..$#selected;

############################################################
# Problem text
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML

## Boiling Point Ranking: Nonpolar Substances

The substances shown are nonpolar. Their strongest intermolecular force is London dispersion forces.

Use molecular size, number of electrons, and polarizability to rank the boiling points of the substances shown.

For each substance, choose the correct boiling point rank.

[`1`] = highest boiling point

[`2`] = middle boiling point

[`3`] = lowest boiling point

[#
[.Substance.]
[.Boiling point rank.]*{ headerrow => 1 }

[.[`\mathrm{[$selected[0]->{formula}]}`].]
[.[_]{$rankMenus[0]}.]*

[.[`\mathrm{[$selected[1]->{formula}]}`].]
[.[_]{$rankMenus[1]}.]*

[.[`\mathrm{[$selected[2]->{formula}]}`].]
[.[_]{$rankMenus[2]}.]*

#]{align => '|c|c|', horizontalrules => 1}

END_PGML

############################################################
# Answer checkers
############################################################

for my $i (0..$#selected) {
  ANS($rankMenus[$i]->cmp());
}

############################################################
# Hint
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_HINT

All of the substances in this problem are nonpolar, so their strongest intermolecular force is London dispersion forces.

London dispersion forces get stronger when particles have more electrons and larger, more polarizable electron clouds.

For similar nonpolar substances:

larger particle size usually means stronger London dispersion forces.

stronger London dispersion forces usually means a higher boiling point.

So, rank the substances from largest particle or most electrons to smallest particle or fewest electrons.

END_PGML_HINT

############################################################
# Solution
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

The correct order is:

[#
[.Boiling point rank.]
[.Substance.]
[.Reason.]*{ headerrow => 1 }

[.1 - highest boiling point.]
[.[``\mathrm{[$selected[$highest_indices[0]]->{formula}]}``].]
[.[$selected[$highest_indices[0]]->{reason}].]*

[.2 - middle boiling point.]
[.[``\mathrm{[$selected[$middle_indices[0]]->{formula}]}``].]
[.[$selected[$middle_indices[0]]->{reason}].]*

[.3 - lowest boiling point.]
[.[``\mathrm{[$selected[$lowest_indices[0]]->{formula}]}``].]
[.[$selected[$lowest_indices[0]]->{reason}].]*

#]{align => '|c|c|l|', horizontalrules => 1}

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();